rtant to  identify where these accessible regions  are if you want to include them improve  prediction models  another important probe in state is dna  methylation and so a recent paper in  grasses showed that if you look at the  chromatin state  across different tissues  you can see that they're all  unmethylated um the accessible regions  and so  what we also know about  dna methylation stayed across  developmental times and it's very stable  and so the suggestion is that if we can  create dna methylation in a specific  tissue then that could potentially  capture the global potentially excessive  space across all tissues  so  predicting chromatin accessibility and  dna manipulation directly from sequence  we have a number of different benefits  the first one is a sequence based model  could be queried to highlight functional  sites and ranking yields it could be  similar to a verb score and really just  serve to narrow down to this top x  variance based on effect size  the second important point is if we get  good cross species models then we'd be  able to predict regulatory regions and  species that we haven't done these  assays and we only have genome  assemblies  this could save time and money by  avoiding is relatively expensive from  its masses  so we know that these accessible regions  are expected to be enriched in  transcription factor binding sites and  so we might use that as a signal to  separately accessible for inaccessible  but unfortunately it's a very noisy  signal because we observe that there's a  lot of spurious matches to transcription  fiber binding sites and inaccessible  regions that aren't functional  so  my hypothesis was that maybe it's really  the order  relative orientation of these motifs  that serve as a stronger signal to help  differentiate accessible from  inaccessible  and what we also know is that these  transcription factor binding domains are  conserved across long evolutionary time  periods and so if we find location one  species we expect them to be observable  also in other species as well for  transcription factor  and so uh convolutional neural networks  have already been shown to be able to  predict chromatin state uh from sequence  within species at least in humans  so we see at the bottom of dc which is a  pretty standard convolutional neural  network which is one of the first  applied to try and predict this problem  and it gets a range of word known as  aprs you can just think of as a metric  for classification performance from zero  to point eight  and then  the year after that dnq was released  which is a recurrent convolutional  neural network which shows improvement  across almost all the test cases  um  the chromatin states and the tissues  that they've tested  um and so my question was can we then  use  dnq to  prevent chromatin accessibility across  species  so  the data they used to train the model  was ataxia data which basically causes  re-pilots underneath the accessible  regions of the genome  then you can take what's known as a peak  color to then binarize this output and  get binary  accessible sequence and accessible  sequence regions  and then i take what's known as umrs  which  you do methylation sequencing on the  genome and you get a methylation state  for all three contexts of dna  methylation you say if it's absent in  all three contexts  and so then again i have  binary on methylated and  methylated freedom  so in total i have training data from  about 12 different species for ataxi  spread nice and evenly across the  angiosperms  for the umr we have about 10 species  five of which are from the original  grasses paper and the five of which are  newly called for this word in the  bodybox  so just a quick overview of how i'm  going to model this i'm taking in 600  base pairs of sequence and i'm centering  it on the  umr the attack cpp and if it's shorter  than that i'll extend symmetrically from  the midpoint  i'll take it the name q which is that  recurrent 